= A1
{c}
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Work in the dilute, single-scattering limit and add the diffuse scattered <light intensities> of randomly positioned particles. The incident <unpolarized light> has equal, mutually uncorrelated $x$ and $y$ electric components. Each particle therefore has an induced <electric dipole moment> along its permitted axis, with the same mean squared magnitude proportional to $|\alpha|^2$.

For an observer in direction $\widehat{\mathbf n}=(\sin\psi,0,\cos\psi)$, choose transverse unit directions $\mathbf e_\parallel=(\cos\psi,0,-\sin\psi)$ in the scattering plane and $\mathbf e_\perp=\widehat{\mathbf y}$ perpendicular to it. The <electric dipole radiation> field is proportional to the transverse projection of the <electric dipole>. Hence an $x$ <electric dipole> contributes a <wave amplitude> factor $\cos\psi$ along $\mathbf e_\parallel$, while a $y$ <electric dipole> contributes its full <wave amplitude> along $\mathbf e_\perp$.

Let $C>0$ collect the common incident <light intensity>, <electric polarizability>, distance and <frequency> factors. Then
$$
I_\parallel=CN_1\cos^2\psi,\qquad I_\perp=CN_2.
$$
The two components are uncorrelated, so the <coherency matrix> is diagonal in this basis. The <degree of polarization> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal P_{xz}(\psi)=\frac{|I_\perp-I_\parallel|}{I_\perp+I_\parallel}
=\frac{|N_2-N_1\cos^2\psi|}{N_2+N_1\cos^2\psi}.}
$$
A signed degree of <linear polarization>, defined positive perpendicular to the scattering plane, has the same expression without the absolute value. The dominant direction is $\widehat{\mathbf y}$ when $N_2>N_1\cos^2\psi$, and $\mathbf e_\parallel$ when the inequality reverses. If the denominator vanishes there is no scattered signal and its <degree of polarization> is undefined. A coherent exactly forward component, including the transmitted incident beam, is not part of this diffuse-intensity calculation; random particle positions do not by themselves destroy forward <optical coherence>.

= b
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Now take $\widehat{\mathbf n}=(0,\sin\psi,\cos\psi)$, with the same angle to the incident $z$ direction. The perpendicular direction is $\widehat{\mathbf x}$ and the in-plane transverse direction is $(0,\cos\psi,-\sin\psi)$. The roles of the two <electric dipole> populations interchange: $x$ <electric dipoles> radiate at full strength, whereas the observed <wave amplitude> from a $y$ <electric dipole> is reduced by $\cos\psi$.

Thus $I_\perp=CN_1$ and $I_\parallel=CN_2\cos^2\psi$, giving
$$
\boxed{\mathcal P_{yz}(\psi)=\frac{|N_1-N_2\cos^2\psi|}{N_1+N_2\cos^2\psi}.}
$$
This is also obtained by the symmetry $x\leftrightarrow y$, $N_1\leftrightarrow N_2$ in part (a). For $N_1=N_2$, both planes give the familiar <Rayleigh scattering> <degree of polarization> $\sin^2\psi/(1+\cos^2\psi)$: the diffuse signal is unpolarized forward and fully linearly polarized at right angles. At a dark direction with zero denominator, no <degree of polarization> can be measured.

= A2
{c}
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The transmitting <antenna> is matched to its input <transmission line>, so its real terminal <electrical resistance> is $Z_i$. The identical receiving <antenna> has the same <electrical impedance> at the operating <frequency>. Its available received <power> for a matched load is the incident <energy flux> times its <antenna effective area>:
$$
P_{\rm av}=\frac{P_iG_a}{4\pi d^2}A_e
=P_i\left(\frac{G_a\lambda}{4\pi d}\right)^2\equiv P_i\mathcal F.
$$
The real receiving load is $Z_t$, so its voltage-wave <reflection coefficient> relative to $Z_i$ is $\Gamma_L=(Z_t-Z_i)/(Z_t+Z_i)$. The fraction of available <power> delivered to the output line is
$$
1-|\Gamma_L|^2=\frac{4Z_iZ_t}{(Z_i+Z_t)^2}.
$$
Consequently, neglecting path losses, mismatch of <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> and line losses,
$$
\boxed{P_t=P_i\left(\frac{G_a\lambda}{4\pi d}\right)^2
\frac{4Z_iZ_t}{(Z_i+Z_t)^2}.}
$$
This is the <Friis transmission equation> with a receiving impedance-mismatch factor. For fixed $P_i,G_a,d,Z_i,Z_t$, it scales as $\lambda^2$. That scaling is conditional on holding <antenna gain> fixed: a fixed physical aperture can instead have $G_a\propto\lambda^{-2}$, and any <wavelength> dependence of the impedances also enters the expression. The matched-output check $Z_t=Z_i$ gives $P_t=P_i\mathcal F$.

= b
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Distinguish reflection at the input terminal, reflection at the receiving load, and a returned signal that traverses the free-space link twice. The input <antenna> is initially matched, so its direct terminal reflection is zero.

In the usual weak-coupling, matched-port <two-port network> model for the <antenna> pair, let the forward and reverse <wave amplitude> transfer coefficients be $t_{21}$ and $t_{12}$. <Electromagnetic reciprocity> gives equal <power> transfer factors $|t_{21}|^2=|t_{12}|^2=\mathcal F$, where $\mathcal F=(G_a\lambda/(4\pi d))^2$ from part (a). The receiving-load reflection returns <wave amplitude> $t_{12}\Gamma_Lt_{21}$ to the original input port. The load-mismatch contribution to the returned <power> is therefore
$$
\boxed{P_r^{\rm load}=P_i|\Gamma_L|^2\mathcal F^2
=P_i\left(\frac{Z_t-Z_i}{Z_t+Z_i}\right)^2
\left(\frac{G_a\lambda}{4\pi d}\right)^4.}
$$
For fixed gains and impedances this returned <power> scales as $\lambda^4$: it contains two link traversals. By contrast, the reflected <power> at the receiving load before that return traversal is $P_i\mathcal F|\Gamma_L|^2\propto\lambda^2$. Calling that local reflected <power> the <power> already back in the input line would omit one propagation loss.

The original wording does not specify an antenna scattering model. The boxed result is the load-dependent echo in an ideal matched-port link, with structural scattering and residual input coupling neglected. The actual total input return can include coherent scattering from the receiving antenna even when $\Gamma_L=0$; gains and effective areas alone do not specify that background. If “reflected back” means only the direct reflection at the stated matched transmitting antenna, its value is $0$. These meanings should not be conflated; the $\lambda^4$ result requires the two-pass echo interpretation and its stated model.

= A3
{c}
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A3}

After <field electron emission>, let $h$ denote the plate separation of the <parallel-plate capacitor>, which the question does not specify. The uniform capacitor <electric field> has magnitude $E_c=|V|/h$, and a nonrelativistic <electron> of <mass> $m_e$ has constant <acceleration> magnitude $a=eE_c/m_e$. The sign of its charge affects the direction of the radiation field but not its radiated <power>.

In the supplied nonrelativistic <Liénard–Wiechert field>, $\dot{\boldsymbol\beta}=\mathbf a/c$. At polar angle $\theta$ to the <acceleration>, $|\widehat{\mathbf n}\times(\widehat{\mathbf n}\times\mathbf a)|=a\sin\theta$, hence
$$
E_{\rm rad}=\frac{ea\sin\theta}{4\pi\epsilon_0c^2R}.
$$
The outgoing <Poynting vector> has magnitude $S=\epsilon_0cE_{\rm rad}^2$. Its radiated <power> per <solid angle> is therefore
$$
\frac{dP}{d\Omega}=R^2S=\frac{e^2a^2}{16\pi^2\epsilon_0c^3}\sin^2\theta.
$$
Using $\int\sin^2\theta\,d\Omega=8\pi/3$ gives the <Larmor formula> and the requested constant <power> during the uniform-field part of the flight:
$$
\boxed{P=\frac{e^2a^2}{6\pi\epsilon_0c^3}
=\frac{e^4E_c^2}{6\pi\epsilon_0m_e^2c^3}
=\frac{e^4V^2}{6\pi\epsilon_0m_e^2c^3h^2}.}
$$
The brackets in the given field require the <acceleration> at <retarded time>; the radiation reaches the far observer after the propagation delay. This derivation assumes that <radiation reaction> is a small correction to the <acceleration> and that the flight is nonrelativistic, for example $e|V|\ll m_ec^2$ for release from rest. Entry, emission and impact transients are not described by the uniform-field model. A result solely in terms of $V$ cannot be obtained without a plate spacing or a specified internal field.

= Section B
{parent=2021}
